# SRG-APP-000015-WSR-000014  -  CAT II
# The web server must use cryptography to protect the integrity of remote
# sessions.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000015-WSR-000014
group:           V-206353
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-001453
control_rev5:    AC-17

contribution:    Splunk Web can be required to serve every remote session over TLS with a
                 named server certificate and private key, an explicit protocol floor and
                 cipher string, and a session cookie bound to the protected channel; that
                 is the application's share of protecting the integrity of a remote access
                 session to this web server.

governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL in 1|true
check:           web.conf [settings] serverCert non_empty
check:           web.conf [settings] privKeyPath non_empty
check:           web.conf [settings] cipherSuite matches ^((ECDHE|DHE)-(ECDSA|RSA)-AES(128|256)-(GCM-)?SHA(256|384))(:((ECDHE|DHE)-(ECDSA|RSA)-AES(128|256)-(GCM-)?SHA(256|384)))*$
check:           web.conf [settings] sslVersions not_matches (^|,)\s*(ssl3|tls1\.0|tls1\.1|\*|tls)\s*(,|$)
check:           web.conf [settings] sslVersions matches (^|,)\s*tls1\.(2|3)\s*(,|$)
check:           web.conf [settings] serverCert not_equals $SPLUNK_HOME/etc/auth/splunkweb/cert.pem
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          web.conf [settings] cipherSuite https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/install-splunk-enterprise-securely/about-tls-encryption-and-cipher-suites
vendor:          web.conf [settings] enableSplunkWebSSL https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] privKeyPath https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] serverCert https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] sslVersions https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/secure-splunk-platform-communications-with-transport-layer-security-certificates/configure-tls-protocol-version-support-for-secure-connections-between-splunk-platform-instances
vendor:          web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.secure https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
retired_check:   web.conf [settings] tools.sessions.secure in 1|true
                 reason: "Whether or not the browser must transmit session cookies over an
                         HTTPS connection when Splunk Web is configured to serve requests
                         using HTTPS (the 'enableSplunkWebSSL' setting is 'true'.) If set to
                         'true' and 'enableSplunkWebSSL' is also 'true', then the browser
                         must transmit the session cookie over HTTPS connections. This
                         improves session security. Default: true"
# --- end vendor verification

external:        A certificate signed by an approved certificate authority, rather than the
                 self-signed certificate the daemon generates on first start, is supplied
                 by the organization's PKI; the application can only be told which file to
                 present.
external:        Where a reverse proxy or load balancer terminates TLS in front of Splunk
                 Web, the integrity of the segment between the client and that device is
                 carried by the proxy and is assessed under its own baseline.

citation:        CHANGED for OVER-REACH and for the same sslVersions regex defect corrected
                 under SRG-APP-000014-WSR-000006. (1) The condition 'server.conf
                 [sslConfig] sslVerifyServerCert in:1|true' has been removed. Per
                 server.conf.spec:798-823 this setting governs splunkd acting as a TLS
                 client validating a certificate that a server presents to it, named there
                 for distributed search and deployment-client polling, and its default is
                 false (:823). It therefore decides nothing about the integrity of a remote
                 user's session with this web server, which is what CCI-001453 and this
                 check text address; it belongs to the requirement on authenticating
                 communicating peers. Because the default is false, the reviewed criterion
                 would record a finding against a standalone instance whose Splunk Web
                 sessions are fully TLS protected, and the remediation it implies is
                 operationally loaded: server.conf.spec:1189-1190 warns that enabling peer
                 verification requires [sslConfig]/sslRootCAPath to be correct first, so an
                 assessor acting on this row could break distributed search while closing a
                 row that was never open. This is not an external dependency and has not
                 been recorded as one, because it is an application setting; it is simply
                 assessed under a different requirement. (2) The sslVersions operand has
                 been replaced with the same verified two-condition property test, for the
                 reasons and with the test results given under SRG-APP-000014-WSR-000006;
                 the reviewed single pattern rejected 'tls1.2,-ssl3',
                 '*,-ssl2,-ssl3,-tls1.0,-tls1.1' and 'tls,-ssl3,-tls1.0,-tls1.1', all
                 permitted by web.conf.spec:239-245. (3) Scope boundary stated explicitly:
                 the inventory rows for this requirement carry no server.conf key that
                 enables TLS on the splunkd management listener, so this criterion covers
                 the Splunk Web listener only. That is the honest limit of the row and it
                 must not be papered over by testing sslVerifyServerCert instead.
                 requireClientCert (web.conf.spec:151-165) is likewise left out: mutual
                 certificate authentication is an optional method, not a condition of
                 integrity protection, and requiring it would fail compliant deployments.
finding:         Splunk Web is not configured to serve remote sessions over TLS 1.2 or
                 better with a named server certificate, private key and cipher string, and
                 a session cookie bound to the protected channel, so the application is not
                 configured to the extent the product allows toward protecting the
                 integrity of remote sessions.
